#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[Cyclotomic layers and separated weights]

== The field at a given scale <c11s01>

A CM field is a totally imaginary quadratic extension of a totally real number field. Its complex embeddings occur in conjugate pairs. Choosing one member of each pair gives a CM type $Phi$. A weight at infinity can now be specified by integers at the embeddings in $Phi$, with its values at the conjugate embeddings determined by a purity or central-character condition.

Let $M$ be the conductor of $psi$. At a raw scale $x'>=1$ choose an integer $e=e(x')$ with
$ (x')^(2+8epsilon)<=2^e<=2(x')^(2+8epsilon). $
Taking the ceiling of the base-two logarithm of $(x')^(2+8epsilon)$ gives one such choice. At a sufficiently deep layer the abelian group $H$ indexes the top locations. Write $h=e(x')$ for the layer depth, $G=Gal(E slash ℚ)$ for the full host group, and $F$ for the maximal real subfield of $E$. The group $G$ has two sign coordinates in addition to $H$. With a fixed $c=c(psi)>=1$ the three sizes are
$ n=|H|=c dot 2^h, quad |G|=[E:ℚ]=4n, quad |Phi|=[F:ℚ]=2n. $
The corresponding physical scale is $x=c^(frac(1,2+8epsilon))x'$. Work in a cyclotomic field whose conductor is a common multiple of $2^(e+2),M,q,d$. Its automorphism $sigma_a$ sends a chosen primitive root to its $a$th power. Take the fixed field $E$ of the subgroup satisfying all four requirements
$ a equiv plus.minus 1 (mod 2^(e+2)), quad psi(a)=1, quad
  chi_(q)(a)=chi_(d)(a)=1. $
Here $a$ is a unit modulo the ambient conductor. The first condition retains the real part of a large two-power layer. The imaginary quadratic characters at $q$ and $d$ supply the two extra signs. The host fixes its CM type by requiring an embedding to send the specified square root of $-d$ to $i sqrt(d)$. Enlarging the ambient conductor while pulling back the subgroup gives the same field up to the evident embedding. The cohomological statement later is formulated for every field with this fixed-field description.

== Computing the field from four tests on a unit <c11s02>

The condition specifying the field has a useful practical interpretation. Start with a multiple $N$ of the four conductors. The automorphisms of $K=ℚ(zeta_N)$ form the group $U_N=(ℤ slash N ℤ)^times$. To a unit $a$ associate
$ a mapsto ( [a]_(plus.minus), psi(a), chi_(q)(a), chi_(d)(a) ). $
The first entry belongs to the unit group modulo $2^(e+2)$ modulo its subgroup generated by $-1$. The others are roots of unity. All four tests in the fixed-field condition are equivalent to membership in the kernel of this homomorphism. Galois theory identifies the group of the field $E$ with its image. In particular, the field is abelian, its embeddings are indexed by that same finite group after an embedding in $ℂ$ has been chosen, and an unramified Frobenius maps to the four entries obtained by substituting the residue characteristic. These three conclusions use the kernel even when some of the entries are dependent. For example, a power of a character of even conductor may already factor through the real two-power layer. Multiplying the four possible orders in that situation can overcount the degree.

This description is independent of a common multiple in the following precise sense. If $N$ divides $N'$, the restriction homomorphism $U_(N') arrow.r U_N$ is onto. To see this, lift a given unit at the prime powers already dividing $N$ and take a unit at each new prime power. The Chinese remainder theorem combines the local choices. The four entries for $N'$ factor through this homomorphism. The kernel for $N'$ is therefore the inverse image of the old kernel, so its fixed field inside $ℚ(zeta_(N'))$ is the image of the previous fixed field. For two unrelated common multiples, apply this argument twice using their least common multiple. This observation accounts for the apparent flexibility in the condition on $E$ in the cohomological theorem.

At $-1$ the first entry is the identity. If $q$ and $d$ are $3$ modulo four, their quadratic characters both take the value $-1$ there. In particular complex conjugation has order two in the image. Its fixed field is totally real. Indeed every embedding of that fixed field is obtained by applying an element of the abelian Galois group and then the chosen complex embedding. Conjugation still fixes every image. An element of $E$ moved by conjugation cannot have a real conjugate under any embedding: commutativity would bring that equality back to the original embedding. This proves that the extension of the real fixed field is totally imaginary and quadratic. The CM type used in the host is the induced one from $ℚ(sqrt(-d))$. Fix its specified root $z_d$ and an embedding $iota$ for which $iota(z_d)=i sqrt(d)$. For $sigma in Gal(E slash ℚ)$, the embedding $iota ∘ sigma$ lies in the type exactly when $sigma(z_d)=z_d$. The quadratic character at $d$ therefore cuts the $4n$ embeddings into the two sets of $2n$ embeddings used by the place sums. Complex conjugation exchanges those sets. Among the two quadratic coordinates in the displayed kernel map, it is the one at $d$ that makes the sign test in the prime sum. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.RamificationFrobenius.TotientGaloisClasses.ConjEMemHostFIff")[The specified root, the CM type and the two degree factors.]

#example[A sixteen-element group computed from residue classes][Take the conductorial example $e=2$, the trivial original character, $q=3$, and $d=23$. The modulus $16 dot 3 dot 23$ has coprime factors. The first coordinate has order four and the two quadratic signs can be chosen independently, giving degree sixteen. A subfield description is
$ ℚ(zeta_(16)+zeta_(16)^(-1),sqrt(-3),sqrt(-23)). $
The real two-power part has degree four. The product of the last two generators gives, up to sign, the real square root of $69$. Conjugation fixes the first generator and changes both square-root signs. At $p=5$ the residue class of five has order four modulo sixteen up to sign. The Legendre symbols modulo three and twenty-three are both $-1$, so this Frobenius has order four in the product group. At $17$ the first coordinate is trivial and both symbols are $-1$. This Frobenius equals complex conjugation and has order two. Here $n=4$, the CM type has eight embeddings, and the degree of the full field is sixteen. This finite example illustrates the fixed-field condition. The later host-existence theorem expressly requires $q!=3$, which would also exclude the root of unity of order three present here. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.FieldChi")[Restrictions on the auxiliary host primes.]]

This explicit field calculation also distinguishes three sizes that occur later. The conductor is an integer suitable for writing the characters. The degree counts embeddings and enters sums of local logarithms. The order of one particular Frobenius gives the length of its orbit on the embeddings. A large conductor can coexist with a small orbit. At a prime dividing the conductor, the correct replacement for an element is a Frobenius coset modulo inertia. The order estimates below concern unramified elements in the two-power factor.

The residue-class computation also gives the Euler factor of the field zeta function in this particular example. Let an unramified prime $p$ have Frobenius of order $r$ in a Galois group of order $16$. Each orbit of Frobenius on the sixteen embeddings has length $r$, as translation by its $h$th power fixes a point precisely when that power is the identity. There are $frac(16,r)$ orbits, corresponding to primes of residue degree $r$. The local factor with $T=p^(-s)$ is therefore $(1-T^r)^(-frac(16,r))$. In the permutation representation, the matrix of Frobenius is a sum of disjoint cycles. On a cycle of length $r$ its characteristic polynomial is $X^r-1$, giving the same determinant factor directly.

For $p=5$ the factor is $(1-T^4)^(-4)$. There are four prime ideals above five, each of norm $5^4$. For $p=17$ it is $(1-T^2)^(-8)$, since there are eight primes of norm $17^2$. The trace of the $j$th Frobenius power in this representation is sixteen if $r$ divides $j$ and zero otherwise. This is precisely the coefficient in the logarithmic derivative $T frac(d,dif T)log((1-T^r)^(-frac(16,r)))$ at $T^j$. It provides a direct check relating an orbit count to a prime-power coefficient. A weighted character projection later in the proof replaces the indicator of the identity in this trace by a character value. Its Frobenius argument and the exponent of $p$ still have to be the same in both descriptions.

== A valuation calculation for short orbits <c11s03>

Write $C_e$ for the quotient of the units modulo $2^(e+2)$ by $plus.minus 1$. For $e>=1$ the class of five generates $C_e$. One direct proof begins with
$ v_(2)(5^(2^j)-1)=j+2 quad (j>=0). $
At $j=0$ this is $v_(2)(4)=2$. In passing to $j+1$ the factor $5^(2^j)+1$ is two modulo four, contributing one to the valuation. Thus five has order exactly $2^e$ in the group modulo $2^(e+2)$. All its powers are one modulo four. Consequently none of them can be $-1$ modulo $2^(e+2)$, and the quotient retains that order. There are precisely $2^e$ classes in the quotient, proving the assertion. For $e=0$ the quotient is trivial.

For an odd integer $p$, change its sign if necessary to choose the representative $u$ that is one modulo four. Unless $u=1$ as an integer, put $h=v_(2)(u-1)$. As in the calculation for five, each squaring increases $h$ by one. It follows that the order of the class of $p$ in $C_e$ is $2^(max(0,e+2-h))$. If the chosen representative is exactly one, the order is one. Equivalently, for $0<=j<=e$ the order divides $2^j$ exactly when
$ p equiv plus.minus 1 mod 2^(e+2-j). $
The integer in the modulus on the right changes with the proposed order. This reversal is what makes the estimate effective. To have a small order, a prime must lie in one of two very thin residue classes.

For instance $e=5$ gives modulus $128$. At $p=7$ take $u=-7$. Its distance from one is eight, so the order is sixteen. The square of seven is $49$, the fourth power is $97$ modulo $128$, the eighth is $65$, and the sixteenth is one. At $p=17$ the valuation is four and the order is eight. At a prime $d$ congruent to seven modulo sixteen the valuation of $(-d)-1$ is exactly three. For $e>=1$ the order is $2^(e-1)$. In the host the auxiliary ramified prime has this prescribed behavior in the *two-power* layer even though a different factor ramifies there.

Let $J$ be an integer bound for the order. If a class has order $2^j<=J$ and $p>=2$, one of $p-1,p+1$ is a positive multiple of $2^(e+2-j)$. Hence
$ 2^(e+2) <= 2^(j)(p+1) <= J(p+1). $
Thus there is a lower size cutoff for primes of short order. A second gain comes from summing only the allowed progressions. The pinned estimates, for $e>=1$ and $J>=1$, give uniformly in the truncation $Y$
$ sum_(2<=p<Y, "short") frac(log p,p^2)
   <= frac(10 J^2 log(2 dot 2^e), (2^e)^2). $
Here “short” means odd, with order at most $J$ in $C_e$. The formal count even allows composite $p$, which gives an upper bound for the prime sum by positivity. A related linear-weight estimate sums $frac(log p,p)$ up to $Y$ over orders strictly less than a real $B>=0$. Its upper bound is $frac(2 B(1+log Y)^2,2^e)$. The strict inequality in that statement is harmless once one chooses a slightly larger upper bound for a dyadic class. The two estimates have distinct uses because a single large prime power and several repeated powers incur different local errors. #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.Statement")[Short-order estimates.] #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[Order and congruence refinements.]

One can see the scale of these answers without using estimates for the distribution of primes. Fix a modulus $M>=4$ and sum over $p=k M plus.minus 1$ for $k>=1$. There are at most two terms for each $k$, their denominators are at least $frac(k M,2)$, and the numerator is at most $log(2k M)$. Consequently the square-denominator tail is bounded by a constant times
$ frac(1,M^2) sum_(k>=1) frac(log(2M)+log k,k^2). $
Both series in $k$ converge. For a first-power denominator, truncate at $Y$ and bound the harmonic sum by $1+log Y$. These elementary estimates explain the powers of $M$ in the two bounds. Using $M$ of size $frac(2^e,J)$ converts them into the displayed order estimates. This local calculation is separate from the selection of auxiliary primes by zero-density estimates.

== A large set with few short progressions <c11s04>

At several primes a local condition asks how often the orbit of Frobenius meets a translate of the thick set. Repeated visits during a short run of iterates create losses in the divisibility calculation. One needs a restriction on triples of visits as well as a lower bound for the number of thick places.

#claim[Lemma 11.1 (Behrend construction)][There is an absolute $C>0$ such that for each $K,N>=1$ there is a subset $S$ of $0,dots,N-1$ of size at least
$ N exp(-C sqrt(log N log(K+1))) $
in which $alpha s_1+beta s_2+gamma s_3=0$, with nonzero integer coefficients of absolute value at most $K$ whose sum is zero, forces $s_1=s_2=s_3$.]

#proof[Take digit vectors with $h$ coordinates between $0$ and $D-1$ and encode them in base $B$ with $B>3K D$. For a relation in the encoded integers, reduction modulo $B$ forces the relation in the first coordinate, since its absolute value is less than $B$. Dividing by $B$ and repeating gives the vector relation. Partition the digit vectors by their squared Euclidean norm. There are at most $1+h(D-1)^2$ values, so one sphere contains at least $frac(D^h,1+h(D-1)^2)$ vectors. A nontrivial relation with coefficient sum zero would put three distinct points on a line. A real line meets a sphere in at most two points, and a repeated point forces all three to agree. Choose $B$ of size $K D$, with $B^h<=N$. The logarithm of the loss compared to $N$ is bounded by a constant times $h log(K+1)+frac(log N,h)+log(h+1)$. Taking $h$ of order $sqrt(frac(log N,log(K+1)))$ gives the claim. The endpoint range where this quantity is bounded is covered by a singleton after increasing $C$.]

To pass to $ℤ slash n ℤ$, start in the short initial interval $8L s<n$ and take $K=2L$. Short integer relations among differences cannot wrap around the modulus. Suppose the elements $t+i_1 h,t+i_2 h,t+i_3 h$ of a cyclic orbit lie in a translate of $S$, with three distinct indices in a run of length $L$. Eliminating $t,h$ gives a relation with coefficients $i_2-i_3,i_3-i_1,i_1-i_2$. If the order of $h$ exceeds $L$ all three points are distinct. Lemma 11.1 forbids this. For an element of order at most $8L$, distinct points of its cyclic subgroup are spaced at least $frac(n,8L)$ apart on the circle. A translate of the chosen short interval can therefore meet it in at most one distinct point.

For a finite list of positions $v_i$, $i in I$, translation has an exact first moment,
$ sum_(u in ℤ slash n ℤ) |{ i in I : v_i in u+S }| = |I| dot |S|. $
Each position belongs to precisely $|S|$ translates. The construction uses this identity with weights to control the mean cost of bad positions. Selection of the top set also involves bounds for the number of top places meeting the same short orbit segment, obtained by averaging finite subset choices. The actual top-selection theorem finds a single subset meeting the weighted orbit bound and the coset-share constraints of Chapter 10 simultaneously. On two-power layers of polynomial size in $x$, with $L$ of logarithmic size, the factor $exp(-C sqrt(log n log(2L+1)))$ eventually exceeds $x^(-kappa)$ for every fixed $kappa>0$. This is how separation can coexist with a thickness $frac(|T_2|,|H|)>=x^(-epsilon)$.

== From a shell of digit vectors to a cyclic window <c11s05>

It pays to work through the point at which the construction of Lemma 11.1 changes from Euclidean geometry into finite group arithmetic. Suppose the digit vector is $v=(v_0,dots,v_(h-1))$, each entry in $0,dots,D-1$. Encode it as an integer $a(v)=sum_(j=0)^(h-1) v_j B^j$ with $B>3K(D-1)$. An integer relation $alpha a(v)+beta a(w)+gamma a(z)=0$ with coefficients of size at most $K$ gives a relation between the zeroth digits modulo $B$. The latter integer has absolute value less than $B$. It must be zero as an integer. Remove it, divide the original relation by $B$, and repeat. The argument allows negative coefficients, which is essential because their sum is zero.

Of the $D^h$ digit vectors, at least $frac(D^h,h(D-1)^2+1)$ have one squared norm. If three vectors on that sphere satisfy a relation with nonzero coefficients of sum zero, one is an affine linear combination of the other two. On a line through two distinct points $v,w$ the polynomial
$ q(t)=norm(v+t(w-v))^2-norm(v)^2 $
is quadratic with leading coefficient $norm(w-v)^2>0$. It has roots zero and one. A third point on the sphere along the same line must coincide with one of the first two. In that case the original relation and the nonzero remaining coefficient force all three to coincide. This is the exact use of strict convexity. A cubical shell in the maximum norm, for example, has line segments in its boundary and would fail this step.

Here is a small version in which the geometry can be drawn by hand. Use two digits between zero and three, squared norm five, and base twenty. The points $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$ encode $41$ and $22$. Their midpoint would have nonintegral coordinates. On the next shell, squared norm ten, the points $(1,3)$ and $(3,1)$ have midpoint $(2,2)$, an integral point with squared norm eight. The latter observation is the more robust one. A large shell can contain many triples with integral averages in the ambient lattice. The averages leave that shell unless the endpoints agree.

For the modular argument, represent the selected integers in $[0,frac(n,8L))$. Suppose that during a half-open run of length $2L$ there are three different visit times $i_1<i_2<i_3$. If the order of the step is greater than $2L$, the three visited residues are distinct. Writing them as $u+s_1,u+s_2,u+s_3$ and eliminating the starting point and the step gives
$ (i_3-i_2)s_1-(i_3-i_1)s_2+(i_2-i_1)s_3 equiv 0 mod n. $
All three coefficients are nonzero, sum to zero and have absolute value less than $2L$. The sum of the absolute values of the coefficients is $2(i_3-i_1)<4L$. Each chosen representative is less than $frac(n,8L)$, so the absolute value of the combined integer is less than $n/2$. Its modular vanishing is therefore ordinary integer vanishing. Lemma 11.1 rules this out. Thus a translate of a single thick set has at most two visits in every such run, uniformly in both the translate and the starting time.

For a short orbit one needs a different conclusion. In a cyclic group of order $n$, a subgroup of order $r$ consists of points spaced by $n/r$ in the standard integer representatives. If $r<=8L$, a translate of a set in $[0,frac(n,8L))$ contains at most one point of any coset of this subgroup. There can nevertheless be repeated *times* at which that same point is visited, separated by multiples of $r$. For example, in the group modulo $64$ the step sixteen has period four. A run of nine times starting at zero visits zero three times. The point-count statement admits all three visits. Later a factorial term can count repeated visits to one point, which is why the order bound accompanies the spacing lemma.

The two formulations in the source allow different endpoint conventions. A closed interval of indices $[u,u+L]$ has $L+1$ times but its largest difference is $L$. A half-open interval $[u,u+2L)$ has maximum difference $2L-1$. The arguments above give the constants for the latter with the $8L$ size cutoff. In both formulations differences of times control the admissible relations. The application to the arithmetic frame writes $n_0$ for the order of its cyclic subgroup and $L_w$ for the length of a half-open run. Its set satisfies $4L_w s<n_0$, its forbidden relations have coefficient bound $L_w$, and the division between short and long steps is at order $4L_w$. With the frame size assumptions its conclusion gives $|T_2|>=x^(-epsilon)|H|$, measured against the order of the full group of top locations. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.ThickFrames.ThickFrame2Large")[The host version of the thickness bound.] #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.Statement")[WindowTwo and OrbitOne.] #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[The closed-interval version.]

== Selecting many top places with a low weighted cost <c11s06>

The deterministic thick set controls where a large weight can be seen. Selecting the top places requires a probabilistic calculation with a different random variable. Let $X$ be a finite set of $n$ possible places. Independently mark each place with probability $delta$, where $0<delta<=1$. For a realization $T subset.eq X$ the weight in the finite probability space is
$ delta^(|T|)(1-delta)^(n-|T|). $
Summing by cardinality gives total weight one by the binomial theorem. If a specified set $S$ must be marked, sum first over the remaining $n-|S|$ places. The answer is $delta^(|S|)$. In particular the expected count is $delta n$ and its variance is $delta(1-delta)n$. The variance follows from adding the individual indicator variances because the covariance between different places is zero. These identities require no asymptotic independence approximation.

Suppose a window $W$ contains at most $L$ positions. A loss at a prescribed place $t$ can occur only if $t$ is marked and at least $K+1$ members of $W$ are marked. If $t in W$, such an outcome supplies at least one marked $K$-element set in $W backslash {t}$. A union bound gives
$ op("Pr")(t in T, |T inter W|>=K+1) <= delta binom(L,K)delta^K. $
If $t$ is outside $W$, at least $K+1$ other places must be marked, giving the upper bound $delta binom(L,K+1)delta^(K+1)$. The distinction is economically important when $L delta$ is small. It does not assume that two *different windows* are independent. The union bound can sum over every window containing $t$ and every window missing $t$, with these two costs.

For example take $L=8$, $K=2$, and $delta=1/32$. For a window containing $t$ the expression using the exact maximum number of pairs of *other* positions is $delta binom(7,2)delta^2$. For one missing $t$ the corresponding quantity is $delta binom(8,3)delta^3$. In the first case the event already has one of the required three marks, so only two additional successes cost factors of $delta$. An estimate using three additional successes everywhere would fail precisely in the windows around the distinguished top place. In the other direction, charging only two successes everywhere can count too many possible bad starts when an orbit is long.

Now let $p$ index finitely many data, such as possible prime steps. At each $p,t$ put an indicator for the corresponding bad-window event and give it a nonnegative weight $omega_p$. If the probability of this event together with $t in T$ is at most $delta beta_p$, linearity of expectation gives
$ bb("E") sum_p omega_p |{t in T : op("bad")(p,t,T)}|
   <= delta n sum_p omega_p beta_p. $
This estimate allows the same $t$ to be bad for several primes and the same window to enter several counts. No disjointness is needed. Suppose $delta n>=16$. Chebyshev's inequality gives probability at most $frac(4,delta n)<=1/4$ that $|T|$ differs from $delta n$ by more than $frac(delta n,2)$. Markov's inequality gives probability at most $1/4$ that the nonnegative cost exceeds four times its expectation bound. If that bound is zero the cost vanishes almost surely. Some realization therefore satisfies *both* the cardinality and cost conditions. Applied to the orbit windows, this yields the selection statement of the release with $frac(delta n,2)<=|T|<=2delta n$.

In the actual host one needs the same top set for the orbit costs and the Fourier coefficients. If a place belongs to a crowded window, that window may include marks from several cosets, so independently controlling each coset's internal windows would miss some of the cost. The joint theorem supplies a single $T$ with the stated cardinality bounds, the required bad-window bound, and for every coset $gamma+A_0$ the estimate $abs(abs(T inter (gamma+A_0))-lambda_gamma abs(T))<=x^(-1+epsilon) abs(T)$. Each character trivial on $A_0$ is constant on these cosets. Multiplying the signed count errors by the corresponding unit phases and summing therefore gives the required Fourier-coefficient error. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.ThickFrames.topsChoiceH")[Selection of the same top set with both properties.] An arbitrary subset produced by the single-cost argument above need not have those counts. For instance its entire support might lie in one coset even with a very favorable bound on its intersections with short orbits. The aggregate cost and the Fourier information are fed to different steps in the final proof. #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[SelectGen and SelectTops.] #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.RamificationFrobenius.TotientGaloisClasses.ExplicitFormulaErrorOfHostConditions")[The separate condition on coset shares.]

== Four scales and three kinds of weights <c11s07>

Write $n=|H|$, $L_x=x^epsilon$, and introduce
$ b=exp(L_x), quad a=x b, quad X'=exp(x^(epsilon/3)), quad b'=frac(a,X'). $
A further thin scale is $w''=frac(b',x n)$. These values amplify the difference between weights under the change of variable $y=a/w$. The large weight $w=b$ yields exactly $y=x$. A weight of size $b'$ yields $y$ of order $X'$, which grows faster than any fixed power of $x$. The character estimates used at such arguments need only give a small relative error.

At each top place $t$ an integer $a_t$ approximates $a$ from above, with an error small enough that $|frac(a_t,b)-x|<=1$. At a thick place the weight $w$ lies between $b$ and $b+eta+1$. At the remaining places that enter the comparison the weights satisfy a thinner upper bound of order $b'$. The complete highest-weight arrays distinguish top, thick, and thin positions and enforce interlacing with adjacent arrays. This enables the same values to specify algebraic representations and to approximate a prime sum.

Consider $w(frac(a,n w)-1)_+=(a/n-w)_+$. Changing $w$ by $Delta$ changes a single summand by at most $|Delta|$, and changes none if both ramps vanish. This observation, summed over prime powers and combined with bounds for the linear centering term, controls the replacement of a thick weight by $b$. For thin weights the factor $w$ multiplies a centered sum evaluated at a much larger argument.

== Paying a subpower price for thickness <c11s08>

The density computation can be made directly in logarithms. Write $n_0$ for the order of the cyclic group on which the shell is placed. For $8L<=n_0$ its lower bound gives
$ log frac(n_0,|S|)
   <= log(8L)+C sqrt(log n_0 log(2L+1)). $
Suppose $n_0$ is between two positive fixed powers of $x$ and $L<=C_(1)(1+log x)^A$ with fixed $C_1,A$. Then the second summand divided by $log x$ is $O(sqrt(frac(log log x,log x)))$, and the first divided by $log x$ also tends to zero. For any fixed $eta>0$ we may therefore take $x$ large enough that $frac(|S|,n_0)>=x^(-eta)$. This conclusion has a lower threshold depending on $eta$. It would be false to replace it by a single numerical density independent of $x$. The proof needs a division by the density costing at most one small power, so this dependence is exactly compatible with choosing an exponent *before* moving out on the number line.

A common way that the shell loses density is visible already in its digit construction. Fix $h$ with $h>=2$ and a base $B$ comparable to $(K+1)D$. Using $h$ digits costs a factor comparable, before shell selection, to $(K+1)^h$. Restricting to a shell costs a further $1+h D^2$, up to constants. To keep $B^h$ near $N$ one takes $log D$ roughly $frac(log N,h)-log(K+1)$. Substitution yields a logarithmic loss of the shape
$ C_2 h log(K+1)+C_3 frac(log N,h)+C_4 log(h+1). $
The first term increases with dimension and the second decreases. Balancing them suggests $h$ close to $sqrt(frac(log N,log(K+1)))$. If the suggested dimension is small, a singleton already gives the asserted lower bound after increasing the absolute constant. The point of this optimization is to allow the complexity of the forbidden relation to grow with the problem.

Denote by $delta_1=x^(-epsilon/8)$ the density scale controlling the top set. The number of steps $m$ is ultimately comparable to $sqrt(x delta_1)$. By contrast the loss on dividing by the thick density involves $delta_2=frac(|T_2|,n)$, which is bounded below by $x^(-epsilon)$. These numbers have different origins. The top density describes how many distinct constraints are averaged in constructing the automorphic system. The thick density is the fraction of positions carrying the desired argument of the prime sum. If one simply replaced the latter by the size of the top set in the final normalization, a factor of $n$ could be charged a second time. This is one reason the set of top places and the translated set of large weights are recorded separately in the arithmetic data.

== What two translates remember about a set <c11s09>

Let $C$ be any finite abelian group of order $n$, written additively, and let $T$ be a fixed nonempty subset. Choose a translate $T+u$ by taking $u$ uniformly from $C$. For fixed $a$ the event $a in T+u$ is equivalent to $u in a-T$. Exactly $|T|$ values of $u$ work. The probability is therefore $delta=frac(|T|,n)$. For two positions there is an exact second formula. Write $d=b-a$. The choices of $u$ with both positions in the translate correspond bijectively to $t=a-u$ satisfying $t in T$ and $t+d in T$. Thus
$ bb("P")(a,b in T+u)=frac(|T inter (T-d)|,n). $
Summing the numerator over $d$ counts each ordered pair of points of $T$ once, giving $|T|^2$. Only the *average over all differences* of the two-point probability equals $delta^2$. Individual differences carry the pattern of the positions in the set.

For example, take $C=ℤ slash 12ℤ$ and $T=lr({0,1,4})$. Its six oriented nonzero differences are $1,3,4,8,9,11$, each with multiplicity one. At zero the overlap is three. At every remaining difference it is zero. For two neighboring positions, the probability of simultaneous membership is $1/12$, exceeding $delta^2=1/16$. For positions separated by six it is zero. The expected number of marked positions in any prescribed set of four distinct positions is nevertheless one, by linearity of expectation. This first moment is unaffected by correlations. An argument for avoiding three close hits, in contrast, must use the arithmetic condition on $T$ established by the shell construction.

The same distinction holds for weighted sums. Let $c_a$ be real numbers and put $Y(u)=sum_a c_a 1_(T+u)(a)$. Its mean is $delta sum_a c_a$. Multiplying out the square and using the two-point formula gives
$ bb("E")(Y^2)=frac(1,n)sum_d |T inter (T-d)| sum_a c_a c_(a+d). $
Subtracting the square of the first moment gives the variance. In the twelve-element example, with unit weights at zero and six only, $Y$ is zero or one with equal probabilities. Its variance is $1/4$. With unit weights at zero and one, its mean is still $1/2$, whereas $bb("E")(Y^2)=1/2+2/12=2/3$, so the variance is $5/12$. The difference between these two answers is exactly twice the change in the two-point term. Later a uniform translate average applies linearly to a signed prime sum. A statement about *one* translate or about the square of that sum would require further information about overlaps.

== Why a large auxiliary prime removes finite stabilizers <c11s10>

An arithmetic space at a compact open level can have points with finite stabilizer groups. An invariant vector there is fixed by all the automorphisms of its point. Changing level so that the remaining stabilizer is a group of prime-power order can simplify the invariant-space calculation. There is an inexpensive test for the final step. If $K$ is a number field of degree $D$, $g$ is prime and $D k < g-1$, a finite $g$-subgroup of $op("GL")_(k)(K)$ is trivial. This is the form in the pinned auxiliary theorem. The preceding choice of level is a separate local requirement. The inequality alone does not assert that an arbitrary finite stabilizer is a $g$-group. #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[The exact field-degree and rank hypothesis.] #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.FinalE")[The established stabilizer bound.]

To prove the test, assume the finite group is nontrivial. By Cauchy's theorem it has an element $M$ of order $g$. Its minimal polynomial divides $X^g-1$, which has distinct roots in characteristic zero. In an algebraic closure $M$ is therefore diagonalizable. If every eigenvalue were one, $M$ would be the identity. At least one eigenvalue is consequently a primitive $g$th root $zeta_g$. Since it is a root of the characteristic polynomial of $M$ over $K$, its degree over $K$ is at most $k$. On the other hand $ℚ(zeta_g)$ has degree $g-1$ over $ℚ$ and is contained in $K(zeta_g)$. The degree tower gives
$ g-1 <= [K(zeta_g):ℚ]=[K(zeta_g):K]D<=k D. $
This contradicts the numerical hypothesis. The degree $g-1$ follows by applying Eisenstein at $g$ to $frac((1+X)^g-1,X)$. Its leading coefficient is one, all intermediate coefficients and its constant term are divisible by $g$, and its constant term is exactly $g$.

The strict inequality has mathematical force. Multiplication by $zeta_5$ preserves the lattice with basis $1,zeta_5,zeta_5^2,zeta_5^3$. The identity $zeta_5^4=-1-zeta_5-zeta_5^2-zeta_5^3$ gives an integral matrix whose fifth power is the identity and which is not itself the identity. At $K=ℚ$, $k=4$, and $g=5$, equality $D k=g-1$ is compatible with a nontrivial group. Conversely a coefficient field already containing $zeta_g$ admits a scalar subgroup of order $g$ even at rank one. Both examples explain the appearance of the product $D k$. A condition involving only the matrix size could fail after enlarging the field. Applied after an auxiliary level has forced $g$-power order, the bound replaces each permitted stabilizer by the identity. In the mass computation this sets the reciprocal stabilizer factor to one at that level, while the volume of the level itself still enters.

== Reading the exact weight windows <c11s11>

The input on the weights can be stated without the surrounding automorphic vocabulary. Put $L_x=x^epsilon$ and take $b,a,X',b',w''$ as above. There are three affine intervals for the exponent $w_(j)(tau)$ of step $j$ at the selected embedding $tau$. Write $h>0$ for the common allowable deviation denoted by the weight-size parameter in the release. For a thick place, a top place indexed by $t$, and a thin place, respectively, the requirements read
$ b <= w_(j)(tau) <= b+h+1, $
$ b_t+frac(b',x) <= w_(j)(tau) <= b_t+frac(b',x)+h, $
$ w'' <= w_(j)(tau) <= w''+h. $
Here $b_t$ is a separately chosen integer with $b_t>=b'$. The large endpoint coefficient is an integer $a_t$ satisfying
$ a <= a_t <= a+frac(a,k x^13). $
The power $13$ is half the constant $26$ used in the size data. In particular $0<=frac(a_t,b)-x<=frac(x^(-12),k)$. At the actual ranks this is stronger than the unit allowance commonly used in the approximation step. These four inequalities state what is meant by the locations of the weight intervals. Bounds making $h$ small *relative* to the relevant scales are further parts of the construction. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[The three windows and the top coefficient.]

The arguments of the centered sums now have transparent orders of magnitude. Exactly at the centers of the thick and smallest thin scales one obtains
$ frac(a,b)=x, quad frac(a,b')=X', quad frac(a,w'')=x n X'. $
The last quantity is the cutoff often denoted by $X''$. A prime power above the largest relevant argument has zero ramp at that argument. At a top place the denominator includes $b_t$ as well as the much smaller $frac(b',x)$ offset. Its individual value is retained in the period comparison, which includes an average of $log w_(j)(tau)$ over embeddings. Replacing all the weights by their thick approximation inside that logarithmic average would change the main term.

There is an elementary estimate that makes the relative and absolute errors here easier to distinguish. Let $a>0$, let $0<w<=w'$, and set $R(w)=sum_n c_n (a/n-w)_+$ for coefficients with $|c_n|<=Lambda(n)$. A term can change only for $n<=frac(a,w)$. The map $y mapsto y_+$ is one-Lipschitz, whence
$ |R(w')-R(w)| <= (w'-w) sum_(n<=frac(a,w)) Lambda(n). $
The Chebyshev upper bound makes the right side at most a fixed constant times $(w'-w)frac(a,w)$ when $frac(a,w)>=1$. At fixed $w$ the corresponding estimate for changing $a$ to $a'>=a$ is
$ |R_(a')(w)-R_(a)(w)| <= (a'-a) sum_(n<=frac(a',w)) frac(Lambda(n),n). $
Partial summation of the Chebyshev bound gives a constant times $(a'-a)(1+log(frac(a',w)))$ when the argument is at least one. The logarithm is a consequence of changing the numerator, where each ramp has derivative $1/n$. It does not occur in the first bound. The center of the principal sum has an additional logarithm but its derivative with respect to $w$, after multiplication by $w$, has size $frac(a,w)$. Thus the same distinction also works for the centered sum.

To check these formulas at a breakpoint, consider $a=24$, $w=4$ and $w'=5$. The argument shrinks from six to $24/5$. The term $n=5$ vanishes at the upper endpoint, even though its value at the lower endpoint is $4/5$. The change is smaller than the unit change of the threshold. The term $n=6$ is zero at both endpoints and carries zero von Mangoldt weight in any event. Bounding term by term has already covered the breakpoint; no smooth approximation to the floor is required.

The passage through the cyclotomic character and the construction of the weight arrays solve complementary existence problems. Fixing the field specifies which Frobenius and inertia elements occur in a cosine or character average. The shell and top selections specify which positions the theta derivatives can occupy without excessive repetitions. Choosing the numerical weight windows ensures that the resulting derivative orders test the argument $x$ on a positive fraction of positions. In the next construction these scalar restrictions must be realized by actual integral highest weights and compatible linear maps. Their realization imposes rank and parity conditions that have no analogue in a single-variable prime sum.
The scale of a raw two-power field and the physical scale of the prime sum differ by a fixed dilation when the original character supplies additional degree. In the pinned interval construction write $x'$ for the raw argument, so $2^e$ brackets $(x')^(2+8epsilon)$ within a factor of two. The full order is $n=c dot 2^e$ for a fixed factor $c>=1$ depending on the original character. Set $kappa=c^(frac(1,2+8epsilon))$ and $x=kappa x'$. Then $x^(2+8epsilon)<=n<=2x^(2+8epsilon)$. Dividing or multiplying *densities* by this fixed factor has no effect on the subpower conclusion just proved, after taking a slightly smaller loss exponent at the preceding step. The arguments of a particular prime sum, however, must agree exactly when the analytic and arithmetic bounds are combined. Chapter 18 tracks the dilation, the interval endpoints, and the extra power available in the twist cutoff. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.WinPairs")[The passage to the physical scale.]

